Recording Policy
For every standards-derived warrant, record:

authority and exact standard/scheme version;
authenticated source version and hash/status;
exact clause/page/segment locator;
scope and VLSI applicability;
rights/access limitation;
supersession/status as a new external scheme version; and
change/impact/reverification/revalidation records when the baseline changes.

The app never silently rebinds a claim from one standard edition to another.

Release Gate
An approvable release must have:

at least one immutable item whose canonical record/hash recomputes, plus a non-recursive release-content manifest equal to the packaging artifact identity/output hash;
all graph nodes, including isolates;
only reviewed-accepted claim versions and semantic/concept records present in the exact reviewed graph;
exact sealed non-vacuous claim profiles, same-claim ISF, normative/authoritative typed warrants, the unified cid activation firewall, identity, and provenance;
a current pass/waived verification head against the exact release/version;
at least one current approved intended use for every included claim, exactly one current sealed query/relevance baseline per use, a current matching retrieval result, and a current fit/conditional validation head bound to the use version, user group, operational context, query version, and relevance hash;
a satisfied assurance argument with all required roles; and
no missing/tombstoned item, wrong record family/version, invalid projection/node, or omitted live graph node.

After those checks, finalize-release freezes the complete deterministic release-relative baseline closure in a separate canonical approval-package artifact. It does not mutate the release manifest. The first package for a release has no predecessor; every later package names the ordered current package head, so the immutable chain cannot fork. Only the current package head is eligible for a new approval or publication. The approver is independent of assertion, accepted review, and current V&V executor acts reached through all included claims, including claims backing concepts, assertions, and mappings. Approval inputs are explicit current links and include each current accepted claim review before publication; approval neither writes files nor changes current release. Later superseding use/query/V&V/retrieval records invalidate older package fitness immediately and require a superseding package version before another approval/publication attempt.

cid Admission
Every cid segment and concept needs:

a preregistered stable target ID;
one domain_applicability_record whose target ID/type match the target’s live stable-identifier type exactly;
an exact non-empty locator and at least one normalized typed VLSI facet row;
a declared basis with its required support: available E-DOMAIN for domain authority, available E-PROJECT for project requirement, reviewer for review, and available typed domain/project evidence + reviewer + rule for conditional external use;
an admission_decision referencing that exact applicability record and target;
decision_outcome='accepted'; and
a scope decision whose registry namespace is cid.

The code resolves scope by registry namespace, not a hardcoded applicability identifier. For claims, one activation definition is used by insertion guards, views, validation, review, release, and publication: scope_namespace='cid', a predicate registered in namespace cid, or either endpoint resolving to a cid concept. Any activation forces an exact active cid concept-scheme version and claim-level applicability/admission. Scope none is allowed only for an explicitly non-semantic sealed profile. A later reclassification is a new decision linked through supersedes_admission_decision_id.

VLSI Facets
Facet membership is multi-dimensional, not a forced hierarchy. The accepted facet types are normalized in domain_applicability_facets; JSON is only the canonical envelope, not the release gate:

lifecycle/activity: requirements, RTL design, synthesis, physical implementation, simulation, formal verification, DFT, ATPG, manufacturing/post-silicon test, debug;
physical design/signoff: floorplanning, placement, CTS, routing, extraction, STA, SI, EM/IR, DRC, LVS, ERC, signoff;
artifact: specification, HDL, assertion, testbench, coverage model, netlist, floorplan, constraint, layout, result, fault model, pattern, waiver, defect;
verification/test method: inspection, lint, simulation, assertions, proof/model checking, equivalence, emulation, FPGA prototype, coverage, scan, boundary scan, IJTAG, BIST, ATPG;
concern: functionality, timing, power, area, integrity, CDC/RDC, mixed signal, reliability, security, manufacturability, testability;
evidence role: requirement, assumption, procedure, configuration, expected/observed result, proof, coverage, anomaly, disposition.

Facet membership never automatically creates broader, part-whole, causal, equivalence, or scheme-membership semantics.
Qualifying E-DOMAIN/E-PROJECT support is required for every claim activated by explicit cid scope, a cid predicate, or either cid endpoint. Interface/external claims do not become VLSI claims merely because they use a claim type shared with cid.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

file-tree structure conveys boundary meaning;
the repository should be normalized while preserving existing work;
public text must not blur authorship, authoring, authorization, attribution, scope, or delegation;
agent working material should have an explicit bounded role;
architecture should develop incrementally before physical movement.

Agent proposals, not adopted as final:

the public/work/evidence/private taxonomy;
a particular root layout;
role contracts as the mandatory method for deriving every path.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

Worktree is intended as an umbrella for multiple repository-shaped work directories;
the engineer’s meaning is not Git worktree;
the term is related to EDA workspace experience;
workspace was explored and found ambiguous in the conversation;
worktree plus work unit became the accepted current working vocabulary;
the path later functioned as a compact implementation-domain index.

Agent formulations and limits:

EDA-style umbrella is a concise analytic description;
the current term may remain until a materially better name is established;
vendor vocabulary can inform but does not itself decide the project domain.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

Development can operate above the software/non-software distinction;
its meaning changes with the object and context;
Development and Storage are sibling project domains;
a storage directory can represent Development without containing the whole concept;
UART supplies an engineering Development case.

Agent formulations and limits:

Development is a relation in which an identified object changes through an activity toward an intended state;
agents and tools are usually means, but can become the object under a changed context;
a Development storage view may hold drafts, notes, experiments, implementations, and intermediate outputs rather than original inputs or final deliverables.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

the engineer values an AI assistant as a tool that provides leverage toward the engineer’s goals;
AI and assistant were reported as a parent-child storage arrangement;
the broader AI discussion was not blanket authorization to restructure;
OpenCode Archive Review and UART have materially different AI relations;
lower-level AI classification was intentionally bracketed at the top level.

Agent formulations and limits:

AI Assistance is a useful domain name for tracing these relations;
assistant/ may be a storage representation of an assisting role and workspace;
AI-related work should be classified by its object and purpose rather than by the presence of AI alone.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

use English for new or revised CI-DIGITAL work in this interaction;
uncontrolled language movement can cause terminology drift;
voice rhythm and written artifacts are separate surfaces;
the tested phrase is a rhythm marker, as the engineer defined it, not the agent’s inferred purpose;
voice interaction, local capability, repository action, and Cadence EDA terminology require separate contexts;
repository location does not make all contained material part of Cadence.

Agent formulations and limits:

existing source-language material should remain unchanged until a reviewed translation is explicitly requested;
surface-specific terminology should cross into another artifact only through an explicit relevant instruction;
interaction style is a safer name than cadence when Cadence is an active EDA term.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

source and transformation roles must remain visible;
the historical-pool authorship default is scoped and permits exceptions;
interpretation must be marked and attributed;
the factual record must remain separate from reconstruction and analysis;
session-derived classification must follow the live session rather than existing docs;
every domain repair must return to the source that created its meaning.

Agent formulations and limits:

provenance supports attribution and backtrace but does not alone establish truth or adoption;
a later complete capture can resolve an earlier coverage warning without erasing that earlier report;
lineage should preserve composite contributions until authorship is explicitly resolved.

Current formulation
Established through the interaction:

conceptual provenance traces present reasoning to prior bounded and recorded conceptual structures;
recorded structures function as memory, and their later influence as experience;
provenance of a concept extends beyond bibliography;
reused concepts keep their original attribution and context;
the term is usable now but remains open to revision.

Agent formulations and limits:

the five-step chain from past elaboration to new reasoning represents the relation;
the distinctions from Provenance, Ontology, Memory, Experience, and Reasoning organize the boundary;
a derived from relation expresses reuse without changing the original meaning.

This work proposes to investigate and demonstrate that a major source of failure in complex engineering and AI-assisted workflows is not merely procedural noncompliance, implementation error, or stochastic model behavior, but the progressive loss of semantic relations that connect project purpose to downstream artifacts and decisions.
The proposed central mechanism is a persistent Relational Domain Ontology (RDO) used as the semantic reference against which lifecycle transformations are evaluated.
The ontology is not assumed to be truth, nor is it identified with any particular solver or implementation technology. It is a controlled engineering model representing the currently accepted relationships among:

purpose and intent;
domain entities;
requirements;
assumptions;
constraints;
architectural decisions;
implementation elements;
verification evidence;
rationale;
unresolved knowledge.

The research question is therefore not:

How can Prolog, Z3, or another solver enforce project compliance?

It is:

Can explicit preservation and qualification of relational semantics reduce semantic drift across engineering and AI-mediated lifecycle transformations?

