4. Separation of Engineering Layers
A central result of the preliminary analysis is that several layers must not be collapsed.

Domain reality and purpose
The external system, stakeholder intent, mission, observations, and evidence.


Semantic model
The relational ontology constructed to represent the accepted understanding of that domain.


Property specification
The particular question being asked of the model:

consistency;
entailment;
admissibility;
reachability;
invariance;
optimization;
temporal behavior;
uncertainty.



Formal representation
The mathematical or logical encoding suitable for evaluating that property.


Reasoning mechanism
Examples include:

Prolog or Datalog;
Answer Set Programming;
SMT;
model checking;
constraint programming;
theorem proving;
numerical optimization.



Realization
Possible implementations include:

software;
FPGA logic;
FSMs;
comparators;
procedural checks;
human review;
runtime monitors.

Thus:
\[
\boxed{
\text{semantic model}
\neq
\text{solver}
\neq
\text{verification method}
\neq
\text{implementation}
}
\]

9. Important Negative Result
A formal solver does not establish that the semantic model itself corresponds to reality.
Given axioms \(A\), a solver may establish:
\[
A\vdash P
\]
but this does not independently establish:
\[
A=\text{correct representation of the domain}
\]
Therefore:
\[
\boxed{
\text{formal correctness}
\neq
\text{semantic correctness}
}
\]
A perfectly deterministic solver can produce a perfectly valid result from an incorrect model.
The RDO must consequently preserve:

provenance;
evidence;
assumptions;
unresolved terms;
epistemic status;
revision history.

This prevents the ontology from becoming an unquestioned semantic authority.

14. Core Demonstration Claim
The final proposition to be tested is:
\[
\boxed{
\text{A project can remain locally compliant while becoming globally semantically incoherent.}
}
\]
The proposed Relational Domain Ontology provides a mechanism for making that incoherence observable.
The research objective is therefore not simply to construct another project-management ontology.
It is to demonstrate that the relations connecting purpose, rationale, constraints, realization, and evidence are engineering state that must remain explicitly observable across the lifecycle.

Objectives
The work sets out to:

establish the purpose, fundamental concepts, boundaries, and architectural questions the rest depends on;
explain how an AI-assisted C++ process connects a UART failure to a usable technical account;
specify how concepts and relationships can be organized for navigation within engineering work;
describe a Cadence workflow with explicit simulation, synthesis, and BIST responsibilities;
define the digital control and test questions in the RF application context, including the beamforming direction;
explain how changing state and timing affect the relation between observation, reasoning, and action;
consolidate the cases into a presentation that communicates the approach.

These are O1–O7 in the work plan, where each is paired with an activity, a deliverable, and the criteria that complete it.

Interpretation constraints
A project description is consistent with this common ontology only when it:

identifies the entity being described;
states the relevant relation and its scope;
distinguishes a conceptual entity from its representation and storage location;
preserves the status and attribution of the relation;
does not infer semantics from a filename or directory hierarchy alone; and
leaves unsupported relations unresolved rather than completing them by symmetry or analogy.

These constraints replace the earlier assistant claim that CI-DIGITAL, Content, Session, Worktree, Hardware, and Software were all simultaneously Contexts and Domains. That generalization is withdrawn. The present sources establish the qualified dual role for Session; the corresponding roles of the other entities require separate grounding.

8. Semantic annotation and typed-relation policy

8.1 Node classes
The minimum graph distinguishes:

Source, SourceVersion, Segment, and Occurrence;
Lexeme, Term, Concept, DomainScheme, and FacetValue;
Observation and CandidateRelation;
SemanticAssertion and MappingAssertion;
Definition, Requirement, Claim, and EvidenceItem;
Review, VerificationRecord, ValidationRecord, and ApprovalDecision;
Release, ChangeRequest, and DeprecationRecord.

These classes can first be implemented relationally. Naming them does not create OWL semantics.


8.2 Relation families








Family
Examples
Publication rule




Provenance
occurs_in, quoted_from, generated_by, derived_from
Deterministic or authenticated records; source and transformation identity required.


Observation
co_occurs_with, precedes_within_window, shares_source_distribution
Window/procedure and complete evidence set required. These immutable observations are never exported as semantic relations; a separate claim is required.


Lexical
preferred_label, alternative_label, abbreviation_of, surface_form_of
Language, source, scope, and review required.


Conceptual
broader_than, narrower_than, part_of, contrasts_with, depends_on, measures, verifies
Predicate definition, direction, domain/range policy, authoritative evidence, counterexample review, and intended use required.


Scheme/facet
in_domain_scheme, has_activity_facet, has_artifact_facet, has_lifecycle_facet
Do not convert scheme membership into hierarchy. Multi-facet membership is allowed.


Mapping
exact_match_for_use, close_match_for_use, broad_match, narrow_match, related_match, no_match
Both source definitions/versions, mapping basis, use condition, reviewer, and loss note required.


Governance
supersedes, deprecated_by, approved_for, rejected_for, requires_reverification, requires_revalidation
Decision authority, effective version/date, rationale, and impact required. These conceptual links do not create the corresponding process records.





8.3 SKOS policy
SKOS supplies concepts, concept schemes, lexical labels, notes, semantic relations, and cross-scheme mapping properties. Its mapping links are semantic links between concepts in different schemes, and SKOS itself warns that mapping properties do not substitute for provenance management.22
The index may use:

skos:Concept for a published concept;
skos:ConceptScheme for a governed vocabulary or domain scheme;
skos:inScheme for scheme membership;
skos:prefLabel, skos:altLabel, and notes after lexical review; and
SKOS semantic or mapping properties only after their meanings fit the reviewed assertion.

It must not:

convert co_occurs_with into skos:related automatically;
convert in_domain_scheme into skos:broader;
use skos:exactMatch because labels are identical;
infer that every close mapping is transitive; or
use a SKOS integrity/entailment result as proof of source-semantic correctness.



8.4 Mapping versus harmonization
ISO 25964-2 addresses mappings among independently maintained vocabularies, while ISO 860 addresses harmonization of concepts, concept systems, definitions, and terms.2324 Mapping preserves source autonomy; harmonization changes or coordinates the systems toward common treatment. This methodology defaults to mapping.
Every “almost the same” record retains:

source and target concept IDs;
exact source definitions and versions or authenticated locators;
source and target schemes and authorities;
mapping type;
intended use and prohibited uses;
correspondence conditions;
known representational loss;
supporting and counterevidence;
reviewer and review date; and
validity interval and supersession status.

For example, a vendor’s “coverage closure” and a project’s “verification closure” may support query expansion while remaining unsuitable for evidence aggregation. The mapping is then close_match_for_retrieval, not equivalence.


8.5 Threshold for ontology status
A release may be called an ontology only when all of the following are explicit and versioned:

formal node classes and identity rules;
formal predicate meanings, direction, domain, and range;
class/property axioms and declared inference regime;
cardinality or closed-shape constraints where required;
equivalence and difference rules;
treatment of missing information and the open-world assumption;
consistency and entailment questions the ontology is designed to answer;
test cases for intended and unintended inferences; and
governance for axiom change, re-verification, and re-validation when an intended use could be affected.

OWL’s open-world semantics means absent information is not generally false, and OWL is not a syntax-conformance language requiring fields to be present.25 SHACL can validate an RDF data graph against a shapes graph and produce detailed results, but that establishes conformance to those shapes, not correctness of the VLSI interpretation.26

